** Board themes
Credited in the About dialog to Jose Carlos Martins and Gregor Cramer.

** Country flags -- user interface set, 248 of them
Embedded in tcl/utils/countries.tcl between the markers at line 1101 and
line 4297, which name famfamfam.com as the source. Verified: five of them
(de, fr, it, jp, us) are byte-for-byte identical to the famfamfam set. That
set states: "These icons are public domain, and as such are free for any use
(attribution appreciated but not required)." The About dialog credits Mark
James, so the appreciated attribution is given.

Not documented
--------------
** Textures (tcl/textures/, 220 files, 19 MB)
No statement of origin anywhere in the tree, and the file names point to a
collection gathered from the web rather than a single licensed source:

    WoodFine0001_S.jpg, MarbleBeige0017_1_S.jpg   the naming scheme of
        CGTextures / textures.com, "_S" being their small size (13 files).
        Their terms forbid redistributing the raw files.
    2274499870_9617a0512f_b.jpg                   a Flickr file name
        (photo id, secret, size suffix)
    24f5b46cc7ca935416ef41396840c3a2.jpg          content hash, typical of
        image aggregators
    Diamond_Ebony_Marble.15194542_std.jpg         "_std" is the preview size
        of stock photo sites
    Green_Maple_363.jpg, E-Stone-203.jpg          product codes of flooring
        and stone suppliers
    CeramicTilesPatern_thumbnail.jpg              named "thumbnail" outright

58 of the files carry Adobe metadata blocks, 41 IPTC and 25 XMP, but not one
has a filled copyright or author field -- only tool traces ("Adobe Photoshop
CS2 Windows"). This remains the largest block of redistributed image data
and the one with the least information.

In version 26.09.18-b1, 41 textures (67 files, 3.8 MB) were removed: those
reachable only through the texture browser, named by no board theme and no
pairing in preferences.dat. That took out the Flickr, hash-named and
thumbnail files and three of the CGTextures ones without changing any theme.
The rest stay because removing them would change how the shipped board
themes look.

** User interface icons (tcl/icons.tcl and others, ~1300 images)
No designer is named. The About dialog defines a heading for icon design
("Icon design") but never renders it -- there is nobody to credit it to.

** Decorative motives (tcl/images/, "Motive-*.png")
No statement of origin. The files carry no author metadata, only timestamps
and "Adobe ImageReady".

** Mouse cursors (tcl/cursor/)
No statement of origin.

Country flags -- the rest
-------------------------
What looked like a contradiction turned out to be two different sets. Both
statements were right, about different files.

tools/flag-mapping.tsv maps all 289 country codes the program uses onto flag
files at Wikimedia Commons, with the licence Commons states for each. 271 are
public domain or CC0, four carry attribution or share-alike conditions, twelve
are placeholders with no real flag (At Sea, Aboard Aircraft, Unknown, ...) and
two are organisation emblems (ASEAN, FIDE) rather than sovereign flags. That
table is the groundwork for a replacement, not a replacement itself.

** Replaced (version 26.09.17-b8)
275 of the 289 files in tcl/flags/ and 153 of the 156 embedded square icons
in tcl/utils/countries.tcl were redrawn from Wikimedia Commons sources --
271 public domain or CC0, four with attribution conditions (see below). The
previous ones came from addgadget.com per tcl/flags/readme, a site that no
longer exists and stated no licence. tools/flag-mapping.tsv records which
Commons file each country code now comes from; tools/flag-fetch.sh and
tools/make-flags.py regenerate them.

** Still from the old set: 14 files in tcl/flags/
AIR, ANY, ANZ, ASE, EAR, FID, JMY, NET, SEA, SPC, UNK, ZZG, ZZR, ZZX. These
have no sovereign flag to replace them with: most are placeholders invented
for the program (At Sea, Aboard Aircraft, Unknown, Mixed Team), and two are
organisation emblems -- ASE is the ASEAN emblem, FID the FIDE one, both
subject to trademark rules rather than flag conventions. tcl/flags/readme
says six of the placeholders were drawn by the Scidb author. The rest remain
of unknown origin. ZZX is also the one square icon left unreplaced.

** Flags carrying conditions
Basque (CC BY-SA 2.5), Saint-Barthelemy (CC BY 2.5) and Oman (OGL-om 1.0,
used for both the OMN and the duplicate OMA code) are free but require
attribution, the first also share-alike. They are named here for that reason.

** tcl/utils/countries.tcl -- 34 flags before line 1101
No source marker; these are 16x11 icons in the same format as the famfamfam
set but outside its section. Status unknown, not replaced.

Resolved
--------
icons/ formerly held a "Read Me.rtf" stating the icons were "free for
personal non-commercial use". Such a clause cannot be combined into a GPL
work nor shipped in a publicly distributed AppImage. Whether it actually
covered the files present or was left over from Scidb could not be
established, so the artwork was replaced rather than redistributed (version
26.09.17-b2 to b5).
